M2.4: preemptive round-robin scheduler (kernel threads)

- sched.c: fixed table (no malloc yet - M3), slot 0 = kernel_main on
  the boot stack, up to 3 spawned threads on static 8 KiB stacks.
  thread_spawn() fabricates a swtch context (r15..r13 zero, r12 =
  entry, rbp, rbx = arg, ret = thread_entry); schedule() rounds
  robin-runs from the tick handler.
- swtch.s: xv6-style switch - save callee-saved regs, store rsp,
  load to-rsp BY VALUE, restores, ret. Whole thread state lives on
  its own stack (C frames + swtch context + when preempted, the IRQ
  frame irq_common pushed), so resume unwinds naturally and iretq
  re-enters the thread. thread_entry runs entry(arg) once, disabling
  interrupts and parking if it ever returns.
- pit_handler(): ticks++, EOI FIRST, then schedule(). The early EOI
  was forced by a real wedge: a preemptive switch never returns
  through irq_dispatch's tail, so IRQ0's ISR bit stayed set and the
  PIC went silent forever. Late EOI from the resumed invocations is
  ignored (no ISR bit set).
- main.c: workers A/B print one report each ~every 25 wakes; main
  waits for 6 reports then prints the totals.

Three bugs caught by the dump/trace pipeline, each a lesson:
1. swtch deref bug: `movq (%rsi),%rsp` dereferenced the to-context
   an extra time -> rsp=0, pops marched through SeaBIOS remnants
   (f000:ff53 far-pointer garbage in the dump), ret into the stub
   table -> #DF. New rsp is passed by value: `movq %rsi,%rsp`.
2. Fresh threads enter with IF=0 (they come into being inside the
   IRQ handler); without sti their first hlt sleeps forever. sti at
   thread_entry; preempted threads re-enable via iretq.
3. The EOI-before-switch wedge above.

Evidence: sw#1..8 trace shows strict main->A->B rotation; alternating
"A:/B: report" lines; "sched: reports=6 switches=237 ticks=237"
printed by thread 0 from its own resumed stack. Mid-print preemption
splits serial lines occasionally (seen in bring-up) - console lock
deferred to the console layer.

Also: swtch.s adds a .note.GNU-stack section; isr.s got one too,
killing the long-standing isr.o linker warning.
